    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d111">

                <pb facs="0307" n="265" xml:id="pg265"/>

                <head>111. Telegram From the Department of State to the Embassy in Cambodia<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d111-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1964-03-03T20:10:00-05:00">March 3, 1964, 8:10 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline></opener>
                <p>601. Phnom Penh tels 870–875.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d111-f2"/> Sihanoukʼs draft
                    proposals, which are under study in Dept, obviously contain many unacceptable
                    elements including ultimatum aspect as well as so-called “gentlemenʼs agreement”
                    plus numerous objectionable clauses in proposed declarations and protocol. While
                    documents are under study we believe it is essential that our own suggested
                    draft agreements (Deptel 586)<anchor n="3" xml:id="d111-f3"/> be immediately
                    made available to RKG. This is necessary for several reasons: <list>
                        <label>1)</label>
                        <item>To prepare our position against probable future publication by
                            Sihanouk of RKG drafts;</item>
                        <label>2)</label>
                        <item>To advance for consideration our own concept of a satisfactory
                            agreement;</item>
                        <label>3)</label>
                        <item>To establish a basis for negotiation if a conference should
                            occur;</item>
                        <label>4)</label>
                        <item>To test Sihanoukʼs willingness to negotiate;</item>
                        <label>5)</label>
                        <item>Prompt delivery is essential in order to emphasize that our drafts
                            were prepared independently and not as a reply to the Cambodian
                            drafts.</item>
                    </list>
                </p>
                <p>Therefore you should immediately forward copies of US drafts to RKG FonOff under
                    cover of note, operative portion of which to read as follows:</p>
                <p>“Upon instructions of the Department of State, I have the honor to submit copies
                    of draft documents which the United States Government would propose for
                    consideration along with other proposals and drafts which may be discussed at a
                    quadripartite conference.” Do not ask the RKG to comment on these drafts.</p>
                <p>Certain modification are being incorporated in drafts, including some points
                    suggested by the Thai. Modifications being cabled shortly as well as French
                    translation.</p>
                <p>The Embassy reported in telegram 870 from Phnom Penh, March 4, that it had
                    delivered the U.S. drafts. (Ibid.)<anchor n="4" xml:id="d111-f4"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#bold">Rusk</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d111-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d111-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, Central Files, POL 27–13 CAMB. Confidential;
                    Immediate. Drafted by Hannah, cleared by Johnson and Harriman, and approved by
                    Hilsman. Repeated to Bangkok, Saigon, Kuala Lumpur, London, Paris, Vientiane,
                    and CINCPAC.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d111-f2" n="2" xml:id="d111-f2-content">
                <p>See <ref target="#d110-f3">footnote 3, Document 110</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d111-f3" n="3" xml:id="d111-f3-content">
                <p>Dated February 28. (Department of State, Central Files, POL 27–13 CAMB)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d111-f4" n="4" xml:id="d111-f4-content">
                <p>Inform Dept as soon as you have delivered drafts to FonOff.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
